                <pb facs="1168" n="1024" xml:id="pg1024"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.015251/22</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Armour</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-11-23">November 23, 1929</date>.<lb/>[Received December
                        4.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 10035</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to this Embassy’s despatch
                    No. 10007 of November 18, 1929,<anchor n="45" xml:id="d1036-f1"/> enclosing a
                    copy and translation of a note from the Foreign Office in which it was stated
                    that the Société Industrie et Commerce du Fruit Conservé par le Sucre had been
                    advised semiofficially that it would be to its interest to give up trade-mark
                    No. 148177, bearing the American flag, I have the honor to report that I am in
                    receipt of a further note from the Foreign Office, dated November 21, 1929, in
                    regard to this matter. In this note it is stated that the company has replied
                    that it was its intention to register before the Tribunal de Commerce de la
                    Seine a renunciation of the trade-mark in question and as soon as possible to
                    dispose of all containers and labels on which it is reproduced.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Norman Armour</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
